    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d673">

                <pb facs="0891" n="791" sameAs="frus:frus1934v02/pg791"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">882.01 Foreign Control/771: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Consul at Geneva</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Gilbert</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Geneva</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1934-01-19T19:00:00+01:00">January 19, 1934—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received January 19—3:55 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>15. From Reber. In public session this afternoon Council adopted the Liberian <hi
                        rendition="#italic">rapport</hi><anchor n="7" xml:id="d673-f1"/> in
                    substantially the terms outlined in Consulate’s telegram 14, January 17, 8
                    p.m.</p>
                <pb facs="0892" n="792" xml:id="pg792"/>
                <p>Sottile claimed, however, that the reply of his Government did not constitute a
                    refusal. Simon<anchor n="8" xml:id="d673-f2"/> expressed the hope that the
                    Liberian representative would remind his Government of the unanimous decision of
                    the Council that the report must be accepted as a whole. [Reber.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Gilbert</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d673-f1" n="7" xml:id="d673-f1-content">
                <p>For text, see League of Nations, <hi rendition="#italic">Official Journal</hi>,
                    1934, pt. 1, p. 153 (document C.72.1934.VII).</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d673-f2" n="8" xml:id="d673-f2-content">
                <p>Sir John Simon, British Secretary of State for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
